# Bundler / RubyGems
.bundle/
vendor/bundle/
vendor/cache/
*.gem
pkg/

# Gemfile.lock is NOT committed. `magik` is a library gem, not an application:
# committing the lockfile would pin every contributor and every CI job to one
# resolution of the *development* dependencies, which is exactly the resolution
# a gem must NOT assume — the gem is installed into someone else's bundle, under
# their constraints. Applications generated by `magik new` will commit theirs.
# Re-derive it any time with `bundle install`.
Gemfile.lock
gems.locked

# YARD. Both the generated site and the index it is built from are outputs of
# `rake yard`; the docs published to GitHub Pages are built in CI, never
# committed.
doc/
.yardoc/
.yardstick/

# Coverage / test artifacts
coverage/
tmp/
.minitest_failures
test/reports/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Secrets. Read this block before adding anything near it.
#
# The model is Rails-shaped: `config/credentials.yml.enc` is COMMITTED (it is
# encrypted), `config/master.key` is NEVER committed, and production reads real
# environment variables or the encrypted credentials — never a .env file. `.env`
# is a development convenience only.
#
# Ignore every .env variant, then un-ignore the one committed template. The
# negation line is load-bearing: the broad `.env.*` pattern above it would
# otherwise swallow .env.example, and a deny-list of specific secret filenames
# is wrong by construction — the next variant nobody thought of gets committed
# in plaintext.
.env
.env.local
.env.*
.env.*.local
!.env.example

# The app scaffold ships a .env.example.erb template INSIDE the gem. `.env.*`
# above swallowed it, so it was present on the author's disk, listed in the
# scaffold MANIFEST, and absent from both a fresh clone and the built gem --
# `rake test` reads it from disk, and the gemspec builds its file list from git.
# The negation is the whole fix; the allow-list style above is why it is safe.
!lib/magik/cli/templates/app/.env.example.erb

# The key that decrypts config/credentials.yml.enc. Committing this makes the
# encrypted file pointless. It is supplied out of band: MAGIK_MASTER_KEY in
# production, a file on a developer's machine locally.
config/master.key
**/config/master.key

# Private keys and certificates of any kind. Deliberately broad: a key that is
# only ignored once someone remembers to add it is a key that gets committed.
*.key
*.pem
*.p12
*.pfx

# RubyGems credentials, which have landed in a repo root before now via a
# mis-set path.
.gem/
.rubygems/
.npmrc

# Docker. Named volumes live in Docker's own storage, but a bind-mounted data
# directory or an override file written by an operator does not belong here.
docker/data/
docker/*.local.yml
docker/compose.override.yml

# The dummy app's generated artifacts. `dummy/` itself is COMMITTED — it is the
# aspirational reference application, checked in on purpose (see dummy/README.md).
# What is ignored is anything a future `magik` run would emit into it.
dummy/tmp/
dummy/log/
dummy/public/assets/
dummy/db/*.sqlite3
dummy/.magik/
dummy/node_modules/

# NOT ignored, deliberately: dummy/db/schema.rb. It is generated by the
# migrations rather than hand-edited, but a schema dump is committed in a Magik
# app — it is how a fresh checkout gets a database without replaying every
# migration ever written, and how a schema change is reviewable in a diff. See
# wiki/Project-Layout.md. (No such file exists yet; nothing generates one.)

# Editor / OS noise. `.vscode/` is deliberately NOT ignored: settings.json,
# extensions.json and tasks.json are shared project configuration. Operator-local
# overrides go in the .local files below.
.vscode/*.local.json
.vscode/launch.json
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Claude Code local state. What IS committed under .claude/ is the harness — the
# README, the agents and the commands, which are the repo's workflow and belong
# in review like any other instruction file. Everything below is per-operator or
# per-session and belongs to nobody but the machine it was written on.
#
# settings.json is ignored deliberately, not by oversight: it is a permission
# allowlist and a post-edit hook, so it encodes one operator's tolerance for
# what runs without a prompt and one machine's tooling. Committing it hands
# every contributor those choices silently, and the file is edited by the
# harness itself — it arrives in `git status` as noise nobody asked for. The
# things it used to assert that the repo actually depends on are asserted where
# they cannot be skipped instead: `rake rubocop` in bin/check rather than an
# auto-fix hook, and PUBLISHING.md's guarded bin/release rather than a deny
# rule. Operators who want their own keep it here, untracked.
.claude/settings.json
.claude/settings.local.json
.claude/scheduled_tasks.lock
.claude/.local/
.claude/worktrees/

# CodeGraph index
.codegraph/

# Logs
*.log
